    {
      "id": "library/10665-69384/blocks/prose-sec-001",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Cervical cancer and HPV infection",
      "pageStart": 7,
      "pageEnd": 9,
      "target": "../sections/sec-001-cervical-cancer-and-hpv-infection.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "infection\nCancer of the cervix is the second most common\ncancer in women worldwide, with about 500 000\nnew cases and 250 000 deaths each year. Almost\n80% of cases occur in low-income countries, where\ncervical cancer is the most common cancer in\nwomen. \nVirtually all cervical cancer cases (99%) are linked\nto genital infection with human papillomavirus\n(HPV), which is the most common viral infection of\nthe reproductive tract. There are 40 different geno-\ntypes of HPV that can infect the genital area of men\nand women, including the skin of the penis, the \nvulva (the area outside the vagina), and",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes cervical cancer as the second most common cancer in women worldwide, with about 500 000 cases and 250 000 deaths a year, mostly in low-income countries, and nearly all linked to HPV infection, especially types 16 and 18. It explains that HPV infection usually clears but can persist and lead to precancerous lesions over decades, which screening can detect, and that visual inspection with acetic acid and cryotherapy offer a simpler approach for low-resource settings. It also notes the 2005 World Health Assembly resolution on cancer prevention and control and early vaccine trial results showing near-complete protection.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-69384/blocks/prose-sec-002",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Vaccines against HPV V V",
      "pageStart": 9,
      "pageEnd": 10,
      "target": "../sections/sec-002-vaccines-against-hpv-v-v.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "HPV vaccines contain the major capsid L1 proteins,\nwhich self-assemble into virus-like particles (VLP) \nresembling HPV. These particles do not contain viral\ngenetic material and thus are unable to multiply,\nwhich means they are non-infectious. Two prophy-\nlactic HPV vaccines have shown excellent efﬁ cacy \nprecursor lesions related to the vaccine genotypes.\nFor the quadrivalent vaccine, protection against\ngenital warts was 95–99%. However, because of\nthe heterogeneity of HPV genotypes in different\nparts of the world (Figure 2), the impact of the\ntwo candidate vaccines may vary across regions.\nN",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that HPV vaccines are made of non-infectious virus-like particles and that two prophylactic vaccines have shown excellent efficacy against precancerous lesions linked to the vaccine types, with the quadrivalent vaccine also preventing genital warts. It notes the vaccines are expected to prevent about 70% of cervical cancers in HPV-naive women and that questions on long-term efficacy, boosters and special populations are still being studied.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-69384/blocks/prose-sec-003",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Target populations for HPV immunization",
      "pageStart": 10,
      "pageEnd": 12,
      "target": "../sections/sec-003-target-populations-for-hpv-immunization.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "HPV immunization \nIn deﬁ ning the target population for immunization, \na key consideration is that HPV infection is sexually\ntransmitted and is usually acquired within the ﬁ rst \nfew years following sexual debut. Ideally, therefore,\nthe vaccine should be administered before sexual\ndebut, i.e. before any risk of exposure to HPV.\nCurrent indications suggest that, initially, the \nHPV vaccines will be licensed for use in girls and\nwomen aged 9–26 years. Vaccination has been\nshown to result in high seroconversion rates in all\nage groups studied, but bridging immunogenic-\nity studies for both vaccin",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Argues that HPV vaccines should be given before sexual debut and notes they will likely be licensed for girls and women aged 9–26, with stronger immune responses in young adolescents and possible catch-up vaccination. It describes the expected availability of Cervarix and Gardasil and the uncertain benefit of vaccinating males, younger children, people with HIV and pregnant women. It links vaccine introduction to WHO's reproductive health strategy and its call to use existing services as entry points.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-69384/blocks/prose-sec-004",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Innovative features of HPV vaccines",
      "pageStart": 12,
      "pageEnd": 12,
      "target": "../sections/sec-004-innovative-features-of-hpv-vaccines.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "HPV vaccines\nHPV vaccines have the potential to strengthen\ninteractions among different health services and to\nprovide a new way of preventing a common cancer\non a very large scale, as well as reducing the more\nwidespread burden of genital dysplasia and warts.\nSome of the key features of the vaccines are dis-\ncussed below.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Notes that HPV vaccines could strengthen links between health services and prevent a common cancer as well as genital dysplasia and warts on a large scale, and introduces their key features.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-69384/blocks/prose-sec-005",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "A unique opportunity",
      "pageStart": 12,
      "pageEnd": 14,
      "target": "../sections/sec-005-a-unique-opportunity.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Vaccines against HPV have several features that\nrequire special consideration. Their use requires\nnew approaches to programme delivery; the target\npopulation is different from that usually addressed\nby national immunization programmes; multiple\nstakeholders need to be involved in advocacy and\nsocial communication; and the best combination\nof vaccination and screening for cervical cancer\nprogrammes remains unclear. For the ﬁ rst time, \nsexual and reproductive health communities will be\nworking with a vaccine directed against a sexually\ntransmitted infection that is linked to a fairly com-\nmon g",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that HPV vaccines need new delivery approaches because they target adolescents rather than infants, involve many stakeholders and bring immunization programmes into the sensitive area of sexual health. It describes the Global Immunization Vision and Strategy's aim to extend immunization to other age groups and the hard priority choices cancer programmes face. It sees the vaccines as a chance to promote delayed sexual debut and condom use, which will depend on strong partnerships between programmes.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-69384/blocks/prose-sec-006",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "An expensive product",
      "pageStart": 14,
      "pageEnd": 14,
      "target": "../sections/sec-006-an-expensive-product.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Although the price of HPV vaccines for developing\ncountries is not yet known, it is likely to be sub-\nstantially higher than the traditional EPI vaccines, at\nleast initially, even with differential pricing between\ndeveloped- and developing-country markets.\nDiscussions are under way to obtain access to\ninternational ﬁ nancing mechanisms (e.g. through \nthe Global Alliance for Vaccines and Immuniza-\ntion – GAVI), which could potentially subsidize the \nvaccine for low-resource settings until mature,\naffordable prices are achieved. The additional cost\nof including HPV vaccines in national immunizat",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Notes that HPV vaccines are likely to cost much more than traditional EPI vaccines and that international financing, for example through GAVI, is being discussed. It warns that cost should not be the only criterion and that private-sector introduction among affluent groups could widen health inequities.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-69384/blocks/prose-sec-007",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Introduction challenges",
      "pageStart": 14,
      "pageEnd": 15,
      "target": "../sections/sec-007-introduction-challenges.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The service delivery strategy and promotion of HPV \nvaccines will need to be based on country-spe-\nciﬁ c considerations of what is affordable, feasible \nand culturally acceptable. Vaccinating young girls\nagainst cancer, and other genital diseases, caused\nby a sexually transmitted infection may be a sensi-\ntive issue in some countries and cultural settings.\nIt will be essential to avoid a harmful backlash\nagainst sexual and reproductive health and adoles-\ncent health programmes (see section on Advocacy,\ninformation and communication, page 10), and to\nensure that complementary messages about del",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Stresses that HPV vaccine delivery must be affordable, feasible and culturally acceptable and that backlash against sexual and reproductive health programmes must be avoided. It calls for clear complementary messages on delaying sex, condom use, HIV prevention and screening, and hopes the vaccine will spur cervical screening where it is lacking.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-69384/blocks/prose-sec-008",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Not business as usual",
      "pageStart": 15,
      "pageEnd": 16,
      "target": "../sections/sec-008-not-business-as-usual.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The unique features and characteristics of the HPV \nvaccines mean that all the concerned programmes\nin each country will be challenged to ﬁ nd new ways \nof working together. Moreover, as mentioned above,\npublic programmes will need to act in concert with\nthe private sector. \nAt international level, partnerships need to be\nestablished now to try to reduce the usual lag time\nbetween formal registration and availability of\nhealth-care products in developed countries and\nestablishment of a negotiated price and adequate\nproduction capacity to supply developing countries.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "stale",
        "text": "Argues that programmes must find new ways of working together and with the private sector, and that international partnerships are needed to shorten delays in vaccine access for developing countries. It highlights the challenge of balanced communication about the vaccines' benefits and limits and of presenting them within a wider strategy to prevent cervical cancer deaths.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-69384/blocks/prose-sec-009",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Advocacy, information and communication",
      "pageStart": 16,
      "pageEnd": 17,
      "target": "../sections/sec-009-advocacy-information-and-communication.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "communication \nDeveloping advocacy, information and commu-\nnication programmes related to HPV vaccination,\nfor multiple stakeholders and audiences, presents\nspeciﬁ c challenges and opportunities. One chal-\nlenge is to produce a balanced and informative\npublic education and communication programme\nabout HPV, cervical cancer, and other HPV-related\ncancers and diseases, and to explain the beneﬁ ts \nand limitations of the vaccines. Managing expec-\ntations and addressing the concerns of different\nstakeholders may also be challenging. Policy guid-\nance will be needed on: how the HPV vaccines\nrelate",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "stale",
        "text": "Sets out key messages for presenting HPV vaccines, such as HPV causing over 99% of cervical cancers, being very common and highly transmissible, and often having no symptoms. It notes the vaccines' high efficacy in HPV-naive young women and stresses explaining clearly what they can and cannot do, alongside lifestyle information for young people.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-69384/blocks/prose-sec-010",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Action points",
      "pageStart": 17,
      "pageEnd": 19,
      "target": "../sections/sec-010-action-points.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Key actions related to advocacy, information and\ncommunication include the following:\n• Interaction between those responsible for\nadvocacy, service delivery and stewardship is\nimportant, especially to generate the evidence\nand country-speciﬁ c information (e.g. on cervi-\ncal cancer burden, HPV strains circulating in the\ncountry, age of sexual debut) needed to support\npolicy and programmes (see section on Stew-\nardship and ﬁ nancing, page 17).\n• A communication strategy on cervical cancer\ncontrol in general, and the HPV vaccines in par-\nticular, should target key stakeholders and deci-\nsion-mak",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Lists action points on advocacy, information and communication, such as gathering country data on cancer burden and HPV strains, tailoring communication strategies to stakeholders, decision-makers and communities, and basing health promotion on local needs. It also covers developing materials and training for health workers and addressing financing questions such as insurance coverage. It urges using HPV vaccination to strengthen existing programmes for adolescents, such as school-based education on sexual health, nutrition, tobacco and HIV.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-69384/blocks/prose-sec-011",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Service delivery",
      "pageStart": 19,
      "pageEnd": 19,
      "target": "../sections/sec-011-service-delivery.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
