    {
      "id": "library/10665-69384/blocks/prose-sec-012",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Reaching target populations",
      "pageStart": 19,
      "pageEnd": 20,
      "target": "../sections/sec-012-reaching-target-populations.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "populations\nThe vaccine licences will probably establish an age\nrange of 9–26 years for the target population. This\nage range will need to be subdivided into two cat-\negories: the primary group and the catch-up group.\nPrimary target population\nThis will most likely be girls aged 9–13 years, the\nintention being to reach them prior to sexual debut.\nEach country will need to identify the most con-\nvenient and effective way of reaching adolescents\nbefore they are likely to have begun their sexual\nlife.\nIf the new vaccine is to be introduced within an\nexisting school-based immunization programme,\ni",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-69384/blocks/prose-sec-013",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Partnerships between programmes",
      "pageStart": 20,
      "pageEnd": 21,
      "target": "../sections/sec-013-partnerships-between-programmes.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "programmes\nProgramme approaches will need to be planned\nand organized, making the most cost-efﬁ cient \nuse of existing or, where necessary, strengthened\nservices. HPV vaccine delivery should be built on\nstructures already in place at the national level,\nfor example, the vaccine cold chain infrastruc-\nture. This will maximize cost-efﬁ ciency and avoid \nwastage and duplication, which countries can ill\nafford. While it is likely that, in many countries, the\nnational immunization programme will take the\nlead in introducing the HPV vaccine, partnerships\nwill be needed with sexual and reproductive h",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Recommends building HPV vaccine delivery on existing structures such as the cold chain, with the immunization programme likely leading in partnership with sexual and reproductive health, adolescent health, cancer control and HIV programmes. It also calls for links with education services, civil society and the private sector.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-69384/blocks/prose-sec-014",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Monitoring and evaluation",
      "pageStart": 21,
      "pageEnd": 21,
      "target": "../sections/sec-014-monitoring-and-evaluation.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Although the construction of an effective monitoring\nand evaluation system is a “downstream” activity,\nit is important during the preparatory stage to con-\nsider how to assess process and impact. Process \nindicators will have to be measured through routine\nnational immunization programme surveillance.\nThe impact on genital warts (with the quadrivalent\nvaccine) or on abnormalities detected on screen-\ning – such as  cervical intraepithelial neoplasia\n(CIN) I or II –  is likely to be observed sooner than\nthe impact on cervical cancer. Monitoring these\noutcomes in pilot studies may be useful for e",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Advises planning early for monitoring and evaluation, using routine immunization surveillance for process indicators and tracking earlier outcomes such as genital warts and precancerous lesions. It also calls for adverse event monitoring that captures HIV status and pregnancy, use of cancer registries or proxies, and private-sector cooperation on reporting.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-69384/blocks/prose-sec-015",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Action points",
      "pageStart": 21,
      "pageEnd": 23,
      "target": "../sections/sec-015-action-points.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "National programmes will need to undertake the\nfollowing actions:\n• Develop links between the many different imple-\nmenting stakeholders.\n• Deﬁ ne the primary target population, taking into \naccount the most convenient access points, and\nthe earliest age of sexual debut.\n• Address sociocultural barriers to vaccination of\nyoung adolescents.\n• Determine school attendance rates for the\n9–13-year age group.\n• Update national cervical cancer control strate-\ngies to incorporate the new HPV vaccines.\n• Plan a service delivery strategy for HPV vac-\ncines:\n– Base service delivery for the introduction o",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Lists action points for national programmes on service delivery, such as linking stakeholders, defining the primary target group, addressing sociocultural barriers, checking school attendance and updating cervical cancer strategies. It also calls for comprehensive cervical cancer prevention including screening, treatment, vaccination and monitoring of effectiveness.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-69384/blocks/prose-sec-016",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Stewardship and ﬁ nancing",
      "pageStart": 23,
      "pageEnd": 24,
      "target": "../sections/sec-016-stewardship-and-ﬁ-nancing.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "health system and purchasing HPV vaccines, both\ninternationally and within national health budgets,\nmust be a high priority. Financing for the introduc-\ntion of HPV vaccine will need to be approached\nas part of an overall strategy involving a number\nof partners, both globally and within individual\ncountries, and be part of a general scaling-up of \nresources to strengthen health systems. Some of\nthe necessary ﬁ nancing decisions will be made \nat global forums, or at levels within government\nhigher than the national programme manager.\nThese decisions will be of vital importance to the\neventual i",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Stresses that strengthening health systems and financing HPV vaccine purchase must be high priorities, with some decisions taken at global or higher government levels. It discusses the need for epidemiological data, demand forecasting and lessons from hepatitis B vaccine introduction, and the importance of international funding through GAVI, UNICEF and the PAHO Revolving Fund to expand supply and secure lower prices. It also covers government oversight of private-sector introduction, such as reporting of adverse events and post-marketing surveillance.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-69384/blocks/prose-sec-017",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Action points",
      "pageStart": 24,
      "pageEnd": 26,
